1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,848
Don't scratch — too late, right?

2
00:00:01,848 --> 00:00:05,040
Every itch is a tiny alarm your skin sets off. Why?

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:09,960
Special skin cells called mast cells respond by dumping a chemical: histamine.

4
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:15,696
That histamine docks onto itch nerve endings, firing a signal straight up your spinal cord.

5
00:00:15,696 --> 00:00:20,664
Your brain's somatosensory cortex reads the alarm and demands one thing: scratch.

6
00:00:20,664 --> 00:00:26,352
Scratching fires faster pain-touch nerves that drown out the itch signal — instant relief.

7
00:00:26,352 --> 00:00:32,040
Here's the trick: scratching works by replacing the itch with mild pain your brain prefers.

8
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:37,656
But that scratching damages skin, making mast cells dump MORE histamine — itch gets worse.

9
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:40,824
So don't scratch — but it's already too late, right?
